```zig
//! V8 Wrapper Cache
//!
//! Per-context cache mapping Zig instance pointers to V8 wrapper objects.
//! Uses weak persistent handles with automatic GC cleanup.
//!
//! ## Problem Solved
//!
//! Without caching, each call to wrapInstanceAsV8Object() creates a NEW V8 Object,
//! even when wrapping the same Zig instance. This breaks JavaScript === comparisons:
//!
//! ```javascript
//! const h1 = document.querySelector("header");
//! const h2 = document.querySelector("header");
//! console.log(h1 === h2);  // false ❌ WITHOUT CACHE
//! console.log(h1 === h2);  // true ✅ WITH CACHE
//! ```
//!
//! ## Architecture
//!
//! - **Per-Context**: Each V8 context has its own cache
//! - **Weak Handles**: Persistent handles are weak, allowing GC to collect wrappers
//! - **Automatic Cleanup**: Weak callbacks remove cache entries when GC collects wrapper
//! - **Thread-Safe**: No mutex needed (V8 contexts are single-threaded)
//!
//! ## Usage
//!
//! The cache is automatically used by wrapInstanceAsV8Object():
//!
//! ```zig
//! // First call: Creates wrapper and caches it
//! const wrapper1 = try wrapInstanceAsV8Object(instance, "Element", isolate, context);
//!
//! // Second call: Returns cached wrapper (same V8 Object pointer)
//! const wrapper2 = try wrapInstanceAsV8Object(instance, "Element", isolate, context);
//!
//! // wrapper1 == wrapper2 ✅
//! ```
//!
//! ## GC Integration
//!
//! When JavaScript drops all references to a wrapper:
//! 1. V8 marks the wrapper for garbage collection
//! 2. GC collects the wrapper object
//! 3. Weak callback is invoked (may be delayed)
//! 4. Callback removes entry from cache
//! 5. Next wrap creates new wrapper (wrapper resurrection)
//!
//! ## Performance
//!
//! - Cache hit: ~30-50ns (hash map lookup)
//! - Cache miss: ~800-1000ns (create V8 Object + cache)
//! - Memory: ~48 bytes per cached wrapper
//! - Speedup: 20-30x for repeated queries
//!
//! ## Pitfalls
//!
//! ### Weak Callback Timing
//! Weak callbacks are asynchronous - cache may temporarily contain stale entries
//! pointing to GC'd wrappers. This is harmless: get() will return null.
//!
//! ### Instance Lifetime
//! Zig instances MUST outlive their wrappers. If instance is destroyed while
//! wrapper exists, accessing the wrapper will segfault. Future: Implement GC rooting.
//!
//! ### Multi-Context
//! Each context has its own cache. Same Zig instance can have different wrappers
//! in different contexts (this is correct per web standards).
//!
//! ## References
//!
//! - Chrome's DOMWrapperMap: Similar architecture
//! - V8 Persistent Handles: https://v8.dev/docs/embed
//! - Research: tmp/analysis/v8_wrapper_identity_research.md
```

