Test weak callback cleanup, wrapper resurrection, and memory leak scenarios with V8 garbage collector.

## Objective
Verify that the wrapper cache correctly integrates with V8's garbage collector through comprehensive testing of weak callbacks, cache cleanup, and memory leak scenarios.

## Critical Importance
This is the MOST IMPORTANT testing task because:
- Weak callbacks are the mechanism that prevents memory leaks
- If GC integration is broken, the cache will leak memory
- This validates the entire caching architecture

## Test Scenarios

### Test 1: Weak Callback Cleanup
```zig
test "weak callback removes cache entry when wrapper is GC'd" {
    const allocator = std.testing.allocator;
    var cache = WrapperCache.init(allocator, context);
    defer cache.deinit();

    // Create instance and wrapper
    var instance = createTestInstance(allocator);
    defer destroyTestInstance(instance);

    const wrapper = createTestWrapper(isolate, context, instance);
    try cache.set(instance, wrapper, isolate);

    // Verify cache contains entry
    try testing.expect(cache.get(instance) != null);

    // Drop all local references to wrapper
    // (in real code, this would be when JavaScript drops all refs)

    // Force V8 garbage collection
    v8.v8_Isolate_RequestGarbageCollectionForTesting(
        isolate,
        v8.GarbageCollectionType.kFullGarbageCollection,
    );

    // Wait for weak callback (may be async)
    // V8 processes weak callbacks after GC
    std.time.sleep(100 * std.time.ns_per_ms);

    // Verify cache entry was removed by weak callback
    try testing.expect(cache.get(instance) == null);
}
```

### Test 2: Wrapper Resurrection
```zig
test "wrapper can be recreated after GC collection" {
    const allocator = std.testing.allocator;
